{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the Crusader States of the Levant during the 12th century. He ruled the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187, a period marked by significant political and military challenges. Raymond III was the son of Count Raymond II and Cecile of France, and his ascension to the throne followed the death of his elder brother, William.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension to the Throne*\nRaymond III's early life was shaped by the political climate of the Crusader States. Born into a family with a tradition of leadership in the Levant, he was groomed for a role of significance. His ascension to the throne at a young age was facilitated by the support of the Church and local noble families who recognized his potential. His rule began with the consolidation of power and the establishment of a stable political framework within Tripoli.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Alliances*\nThe reign of Raymond III was characterized by a series of military campaigns aimed at expanding the influence of Tripoli and defending the Crusader States against common enemies. One of his notable achievements was the victory over Nur ad-Din in the Battle of Inab in 1149, a conflict that halted the advance of the Zengid dynasty into the Levant. Additionally, Raymond III forged alliances with other Crusader leaders and the Byzantine Empire to counter the growing threat of the Ayyubids. These alliances were instrumental in maintaining the territorial integrity of the Crusader States.\n\n*Challenges and Downfall*\nDespite his successes, Raymond III faced numerous challenges during his reign. The rise of Saladin and the unification of the Ayyubid forces posed a significant threat to the Crusader States. The Battle of Hattin in 1187, where Saladin's forces decisively defeated the Crusader armies, marked a turning point in the history of the Crusades. This defeat led to the fall of Tripoli and other Crusader strongholds in the following years. Raymond III's efforts to defend his county were ultimately unsuccessful, and he was captured and imprisoned by Saladin's forces. His release in 1188 was negotiated by the Duke of Burgundy, but his health had deteriorated, and he died soon after.\n\nRaymond III's legacy is complex, reflecting both his successes in maintaining the stability of Tripoli and his ultimate failure to preserve the Crusader States against the advancing Muslim forces. His reign is remembered as a pivotal period in the history of the Levant, marked by fluctuating fortunes and the gradual decline of the Crusader presence in the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.4352892637252808}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0002300474588992074}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00035315475543029606}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.12737776339054108}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.01582445204257965}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.361645576660521e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.248942322097719e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -2.52720492426306e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -2.1142444610595703}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09833721816539764}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.9980329871177673}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.12200851738452911}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.12261347472667694}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.048975612968206406}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.34835219383239746}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.06927473843097687}, {"token": " States", "logprob": -1.536070704460144}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.825148344039917}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.008107726462185383}, {"token": " Lev", "logprob": -0.08741782605648041}, {"token": "ant", "logprob": -0.0007518087513744831}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.10225414484739304}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00011240804451517761}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.5204343795776367}, {"token": "